\subsubsection*{Tantalum}

Same conclusions as vanadium can be made for tantalum for which migration barrier of interstitials are 
E$^m_{He}$(T $\rightarrow$ T) = 0.18 eV and  E$^m_{He}$(T $\rightarrow$ O) = 0.40 eV (Tab.\ref{tab:em}). 
Interstitials He diffuse hopping from T to T sites.\\
Concerning vacancies, migration barrier is higher for mono-vacancy (E$^m_{V}$ = 0.72 eV) consistent with 
resistivity annealing experiment (E$^m_{V}$ = 0.7 eV)\cite{faber1974}, than for di-vacancy which 
energy barrier is 0.17 eV lower in 2nn $\rightarrow$ 2nn path.\\
Diffusion of mono-vacancy with a He atom involve first a dissociation of He to the vacancy. Then He hops via T sites while
mono-vacancy jumps to first nearest neighbour site. Finally He recombines with mono-vacancy, 
following the 'impeded interstitial mechanism' or 'He-vacancy dissociative mechanism' \cite{Trinkaus2003,ullmaier1991}.
He enhances the migration barrier of mono-vacancy by 1.17 eV.\\
The increase of migration energy due to He is 2 times smaller (0.51 eV) for 1nn $\rightarrow$ 2nn path 
than for mono-vacancy case.\\
Contrary to the vanadium case all paths involving di-vacancy have the same barrier in presence of He, 
so He$_1$V$_2$ clusters can migrate via three ways with the same probability.\\


Thermal desorption spectra experiment give us direct access to dissociation energy we can defined as the sum of binding energy and migration energy 
of He atom or vacancy :
\begin{equation}
    $$ E$^d_{He/V-He_nV_m}$ = E$^b_{He/V-He_nV_m}$ + E$^m_{He/V}$ $$
\end{equation}

On Fig.\ref{fig:ed} the dissociation energy of He and vacancy with most stable He$_n$V$_m$ cluster as a function 
of \textit{n/m} ratio is drawn for respectively vanadium and tantalum. This allows us to determine wether a cluster 
tends to emit a He atom or a vacancy to gain stability, 
i.e when E$^d_{He}$ > E$^d_{V}$, the cluster is more likely to dissociate a vacancy.
The crossover shows the most stable clusters for $\sim$ 0.9 \textit{n/m} ratio with E$^d$ $\sim$ 1.9 eV in vanadium, 
close to experimental result E$^d$ = 1.66 eV \cite{vassen1991},
and E$^d$ $\sim$ 2.5 eV in tantalum.
This is consistent with TDS experiment \cite{Fedorov1996} in which the peaks at the highest temperature were attributed to the
 clusters with \textit{n}=\textit{m}.

